The Apache-2.0 text, which applies to the parts that follow the project below, is in licenses/Apache-2.0.txt.

EasyLiDAR was written from scratch, with one exception: the coordinate-system engine in
crates/crs, described first below, adapts source code and data that are licensed for reuse. Of the
other programs below, no source code is included; they are credited because EasyLiDAR follows their
published methods or documented behaviour, or because its structure builds on a sister project.

- terrio, Qiusheng Wu (MIT, licenses/MIT-terrio.txt): the projections, EPSG and ESRI tables, WKT and
  PROJ-string readers, datum shift grid reader and transformation routing of crates/crs are
  adapted from terrio's `crs` module (https://github.com/opengeos/terrio), trimmed and changed to
  stand alone. terrio itself ports them from PROJ.
- PROJ (MIT): the map projections, ellipsoid helpers and datum grid interpolation come from PROJ
  (https://proj.org). Copyright (c) 2000, Frank Warmerdam; 2004, Gerald I. Evenden; 2012, Martin
  Raspaud; 2019, Even Rouault and the other PROJ contributors named in PROJ's source files. Its
  permission notice is in licenses/MIT-terrio.txt.
- EPSG Geodetic Parameter Dataset, IOGP (https://epsg.org, terms of use at
  https://epsg.org/terms-of-use.html): crates/crs/data/epsg*.tsv.zst and datums.tsv.zst are derived
  from it and redistributed under those terms, with this attribution. They are not the EPSG
  dataset itself and carry no warranty.
- Esri projection engine data (Apache-2.0, https://github.com/Esri/projection-engine-db-doc):
  crates/crs/data/esri.tsv.zst is derived from it, through PROJ's database.
- Datum shift grids and geoid models of Spain, built into crates/crs/data/grids (CC-BY 4.0,
  converted by the PROJ-data project, https://github.com/OSGeo/PROJ-data): ED50 to ETRS89
  (SPED2ETV2) and EGM08-REDNAP geoid, derived from data of the Instituto Geográfico Nacional
  (www.ign.es); ED50 to ETRS89 for Catalonia, derived from data of the Institut Cartogràfic i
  Geològic de Catalunya (www.icgc.cat). Licence: https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/

- RustGIS (Apache-2.0): the LAS reader's approach, the layered architecture and the look of the
  interface.
- FUSION / LDV, R. J. McGaughey, USDA Forest Service (public domain): the documented output formats of its
  ground filter, canopy model, grid metrics and clipping tools, which EasyLiDAR's results are compatible
  with and were compared against by testing.
- MCC-LIDAR, Green Code LLC (Apache-2.0): the multiscale curvature classification of Evans and
  Hudak (2007) and its documented parameters.
- CSF, Jianbo Qi (Apache-2.0): the cloth simulation filter of Zhang et al. (2016).
- lidR, J.-R. Roussel et al. (GPL-3): only its documented behaviour was observed, by running it as a
  separate program; no code was used. Tree segmentation follows Popescu and Wynne (2004), Dalponte
  and Coomes (2016) and Silva et al. (2016).
- OxiGeo, COOLJAPAN OU (Apache-2.0): reference for which terrain products are worth having (slope,
  aspect, hillshade, curvatures, drainage, viewshed). Its source code is not included; the terrain
  analysis follows the published methods of Horn (1981), Zevenbergen and Thorne (1987), Wilson et
  al. (2007), Weiss (2001), O'Callaghan and Mark (1984), Beven and Kirkby (1979) and Barnes,
  Lehman and Mulla (2014).
- GDAL / gdaldem (MIT): the conventions of its slope, aspect, hillshade, roughness, TRI and TPI
  (windows over missing neighbours, units), checked against an independent implementation.
- WhiteboxTools, John Lindsay (MIT): inspiration for the choice of utilities (thinning, point
  statistics, tiling, contours).
- Map tiles of the base map are not part of EasyLiDAR: they are fetched while the base map is on
  from OpenStreetMap (© OpenStreetMap contributors, ODbL, https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright;
  the tile servers' terms are at https://operations.osmfoundation.org/policies/tiles/) and from the
  IGN (ortophoto PNOA and base map © Instituto Geográfico Nacional, CC BY 4.0 scne.es). The credit
  is drawn on the map. Tiles are cached for a week in the user's cache folder.
- GeoPackage reading follows the OGC GeoPackage 1.x format and the SQLite file format, both public;
  its page reader is shaped after the one in terrio (above).
- SMRF follows Pingel, Clarke and McBride (2013); the height above ground uses a TIN of the cloud's
  ground points.
- COPC writing follows the COPC 1.0 specification (Hobu Inc. and contributors,
  https://copc.io), a public format description; no code of other COPC writers is included.
- E57 files are read with the e57 crate (MIT); the format is ASTM E2807.
- The intensity normalised for range follows Höfle and Pfeifer (2007), "Correction of laser
  scanning intensity data: data and model-driven approaches", ISPRS J. Photogramm. 62: 415–433.
- JPEG tiles of the cloud-optimized GeoTIFFs are encoded with jpeg-encoder: this software is based
  in part on the work of the Independent JPEG Group.
- Third-party libraries: see THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.md.

